NAME
    setreg - write a CPU register

SYNOPSIS
    setreg <register> <value>
    setreg <cpu> <register> <value>

DESCRIPTION
    Sets one of a processor's registers to a value. Until this existed the
    debugger could read every register and change none of them, which meant
    a hypothesis could only ever be tested by editing memory and waiting to
    see whether the machine happened to reload the register from it.

    What it is actually for is counterfactuals. Halt on the compare that
    gates a routine, flip the register it compares, resume, and watch what
    the game does down the branch it did not take. That answers 'is this
    check the reason my sprite never appears' in one step, where coverage
    and breakpoints can only establish that the branch was not taken.

    It also skips a hang: forcing PC past a spin loop lets a boot sequence
    continue far enough to show what the NEXT problem is, which is often
    worth more than the one you are stopped on.

    Values are hex, and are REFUSED rather than truncated if they do not fit
    the register's width - a silently masked value would be a wrong answer
    reported as a success. An unknown register name lists the ones that chip
    actually has.

    Kept separate from 'regs' on purpose. 'regs' is read-only, which is what
    lets an always-live shell answer it instantly off the emulation thread;
    folding a write into it would drag every register dump onto the
    emulation thread's next frame, and writing a register from another
    thread would be a race besides.

OTHER PROCESSORS
    A scope word writes another chip's registers - 'cpus' lists which ones
    support it at all. Not every chip does: the GSU and the NEC DSP report
    their registers through debug accessors with no write path behind them,
    so they refuse rather than pretending. 'cpus' prints 'setreg' in a
    chip's supported list only when it really is writable.

    Take care with a halted coprocessor: only one chip halts at a time, so
    writing a register on a chip that is still running races its own
    execution.

EXAMPLES
    setreg a 0007
    setreg pc 8123
    setreg p 34
    setreg spc a 00
    setreg sa1 x 0010

SEE ALSO
    regs, cpus, bp, step, eval
